  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nA surface warm front moved across MN/WI early this morning and has \nnow reached far northern MN. This is why it may not have felt as \nchilly when you walked out the door this morning as temperatures are \nranged 5-10 degrees warmer than what they did the previous \nmorning. Satellite showing eastern MN and western WI wedged in \nbetween two areas low to mid-level stratus. For sky coverage \ntoday, decided to favor a blend of NBM/Consshort/HREF as it is \nexpected that the clear slot should begin to transition into \nmore filtered sunshine for the rest of this afternoon. The lucky\nareas that have managed to remain under the clearer skies can \nexpect temperatures to reach near 70 degrees while areas farther\nwill roughly 5-7 degrees cooler given the lack of surface \nheating. Tonight, clouds will continue to build in from the \nsouth and west which should help insulate our lows in the \nmid-50s. \n\nThe meat and potatoes of this forecast surrounds Monday through \nWednesday. An embedded mid-level wave that is part of a parent \ntroughing pattern over the intermountain west will move in across \nthe Dakotas. This will serve as forcing for the first large band of \nrain that moves in late tonight and spreads east across MN/WI \nthrough the first half of Monday morning. As of now, eastern MN \nwould see rainfall arriving around the morning commute while western \nWI would see rainfall arrive by mid-morning. Winds during this time \nare expected to increase across southwestern MN between 25-35 mph. \nBy the mid-late afternoon, a dry slot will begin making its way into \nwestern MN which the forces rainfall to become much more scattered \nin coverage through the overnight hours. At this time, another \nsurface low develops farther south along the same parent trough \nin south-central plains. This feature than is tracked NNE'ward \nand is expected to converge onto the northern wave of forcing. \nThe act of this convergence should help generate a new area of \nlow pressure somewhere across southern MN into northern IA. \nThis then will introduce wave number two of widespread precip \nTuesday. By Wednesday and Thursday, the parent troughing pattern\ndawdles over the northern plains and maintains likely chances \nfor additional scattered wrap- around rain rainfall. One thing \nto note though is PoPs in the latest NBM appear to favor GFS \noutput with precip ending as late Wednesday night as compared to\nthe ECMWF and Canadian departure. But have maintained 20-30 PoP\nmentions on Thursday to reflect current confidence across \nwestern WI. \n\nQPF rainfall amounts have not changed much since the previous \nforecast. Confidence continues to favor that a QPF swath ranging \nbetween 1-2.5\" of rainfall could fall in eastern SD to northwestern \nMN. Areas within central MN into western WI could see 1.5-1.75\". \nAreas mainly south of a line from New Ulm down to Pepin could \nsee closer to 0.75\". Of course localized higher amounts are \npossible. Rainfall rates are not expected to be heavy with this \nsystem but given the longer duration of rainfall, concerns for \nlocalized flooding/ponding could be possible. \n\nFor the second half of the week into next weekend, the troughing \npattern will finally make its way east leaving at least Friday dry. \nOnce the weekend arrives, guidance diverges in solutions as ridging \nbuilds in over the western CONUS and troughing sinks into the \ncentral plains. The EPS and GEFS both favor another shot at rain \nshowers however, GEFS prefers a track over IA vs over MN currently \nfeatured in the EPS. Still some time to work out the details. As for \ntemperatures for the rest of the week, highs should range in the \nupper 40s to lower 50s with lows in the mid-30s to lower 40s.",
